  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 301 PM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nCold air advection is ongoing following a cold frontal passage last \nnight. Temperatures have been falling through the daylight hours as \nnorthwesterly winds gust to between 20-30 mph. Little additional \ntemperature recovery is anticipated and our highs for the day are \nlikely to have already occurred. Persistent low cloud cover will \nhelp today feel quite miserable after the prolonged stretch of warm \nweather we have seen.\n\nAloft, a potent but positively-tilted trough is diving in from the \nnorth. This feature will steepen lapse rates while propelling a \nsecondary front southward. Lapse rates could steepen even more \nimmediately down wind of Lake Michigan as flow passes over the \nwarmer water, increasing boundary layer temps by a few degrees. A \nband of scattered showers is likely this evening in conjunction with \nthese features. Best timing brings showers to the Lafayette area \naround 5-6pm with activity reaching Indy by 7-8pm, and eventually \nBloomington by 9-10pm. Shower activity could be briefly heavy, but \noverall rainfall amounts will be under a tenth of an inch.\n\nWinds are expected to become northerly behind the secondary front. \nSpeeds and gusts are also expected to increase, with gusts this \nevening between 25 to 35 mph possible. The upper trough departs \novernight and a quick reduction in winds appears likely tonight. \nGuidance is in good agreement showing speeds dropping to under 15 \nmph by sunrise.\n\nCombined with clearing skies, winds may become calm enough to allow \nfor patchy frost north of Indy overnight mainly in sheltered areas. \nOverall, our current thinking is winds remain elevated just enough \nto prevent widespread frost. As such, a Frost Advisory was not \nissued for tonight. Nevertheless, we introduced the mention of \npatchy frost in rural areas over the northern parts of our CWA.\n\nAs surface high pressure builds in on Saturday, the clearing trend \ncontinues with winds becoming calm by sunset. Temperatures likely \nremain suppressed somewhat due to waning cold air advection. Highs \nonly in the 50s are probable despite a return to sunshine. By \nSaturday night, ideal radiative cooling conditions will be in place. \nTemperatures in the 30s are very likely with widespread frost. \nNortheastern portions of our CWA may even dip into the low 30s or \nupper 20s. We have issued a Freeze Watch for these northeastern \nareas Saturday night into Sunday morning. Depending on how the \nsurface high sets up, the Freeze Watch may need to be expanded in \nfurther updates.\n\nSimilar conditions are likely Sunday night with areas of frost \nlikely, especially in rural locations.\n\nNEXT WEEK\n\nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement showing surface high pressure \nremaining in place through at least mid next week...possibly even \nthe entire week. Aloft, troughing is replaced by ridging. These two \nfactors point towards a warming trend through next week. The warming \nshould be gradual, as the advective component is not there. Highs \nrebound into the 70s which is a far cry from the near 90 readings we \nsaw a few days ago.",
